% ============================================================================
% dwarf-planet-criterion-status — for each of the three IAU planet criteria,
% whether a dwarf planet like Pluto meets it, per the SAME already-cited NASA
% sentence (adj-facts-stdlib, ASTRONOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `planet-criterion.adj`
% (`planet_criterion(criterion, requirement)`, the three IAU requirements a
% body must meet to count as a full planet — orbit → orbits_its_host_star,
% roundness → is_mostly_round, cleared_orbit →
% gravity_cleared_away_other_objects_of_similar_size_near_its_orbit). That
% table's own header already quotes, verbatim, a NASA sentence that states,
% for all three criteria at once, whether a dwarf planet satisfies each one —
% a fact used there only to justify EXCLUDING `dwarf_planet` as a row, never
% decoded into a table of its own:
%
%   "Dwarf planets like Pluto were defined as objects that orbit the Sun,
%    and are nearly round, but have not been able to clear their orbit of
%    debris"
%
% The sentence's own structure fixes each criterion's status: the leading
% clauses ("orbit the Sun", "are nearly round") state what dwarf planets DO
% satisfy; the trailing "but have not been able to..." clause contrasts the
% third criterion as the one they FAIL. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? dwarf_planet_criterion_status(cleared_orbit, $Status)   % not_met
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table): each `row`
% lowers to a ground relation `dwarf_planet_criterion_status(criterion,
% status)` carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD
% binding query — the engine returns the status WITH its source, on the CPU,
% with zero model calls, and abstains on anything that is not one of these
% three criteria. Full 3/3 coverage — the single quoted sentence states a
% status for every criterion, so no abstention is needed among the three;
% the table abstains only on non-criterion atoms, the same abstention set
% `planet-criterion.adj` already establishes. The query also runs BACKWARD —
% bind a status and recall which criteria have it:
%
%     ? dwarf_planet_criterion_status($C, met)   % orbit AND roundness
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME NASA sentence
% `planet-criterion.adj`'s own header already quotes (there, to justify
% excluding `dwarf_planet` as a row) — no new WebFetch, only a different
% schema decoding the per-criterion STATUS half of the already-verified
% quote instead of using it as exclusionary prose. `trust authoritative` —
% the same tier `planet-criterion.adj` already carries (NASA Science, a
% primary U.S. government resource).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table dwarf_planet_criterion_status {
    columns criterion, status

    row (orbit, met)
    row (roundness, met)
    row (cleared_orbit, not_met)

    source "Dwarf planets like Pluto were defined as objects that orbit the Sun, and are nearly round, but have not been able to clear their orbit of debris"
    locator "https://science.nasa.gov/dwarf-planets/"
    trust authoritative
}
